text:
@Glazenbol-uo2ni  to each their own of course. I bet many of those cars are better for folks who like the familiarity of the ICE cars they come from. But an entry e35 costs more than the most expensive model 3. Im betting a less exciting driving experience..at least for my taste.
While not a fan of the value for money on many European cars, I can still appreciate some of their ICE offerings. Not convinced their EVs will be great but haven't driven one.. The Mercedes personnel urged a family member of mine to avoid the EQS and one other EV model last I was there. Too many coming back with issues.. Of course this may have just been local..
  I remember when people were gravitating towards Scion back in the day because they thought they looked neat and too many people drive civics and corollas, etc. Im sure it worked out for some of them but I never felt a compulsion to stand out after high school.

@Nickbaldeagle02 Good question.  Insurance can be a problem for people.  My insurance on my 2023 Model 3 RWD is about the same as it was on my 2019 Honda Passport, after a little shopping around.  I imagine something like this would be a little bit more. 

I had Geico and was paying $480 every six months for the Passport.  They quoted me an additional $1400 or so to add the Model 3.  I remember State Farm quoted me something like $2400 for just the Model 3.  I initially went with Tesla insurance, which was cheap at $40-$60 per month based on my driving, but I didn't like the way it worked.  I ended up going with Progressive for $400 the first six months and $500 every six months after that.  

I know a lot of people get quoted crazy high rates, I did too, but I wonder how often they don't shop around.  This doesn't just apply to EVs either.  When I was younger I just had Nationwide forever.  One day I decided to ask my agent about it, mid 2000's I think this was, and she was able to switch me over to Geico for around 1/3rd of the price.  Obviously EVs are more expensive on average for a number of reasons but it always pays to shop around.

