@whodatcatt if you're getting 50k out of tires you aren't driving that vehicle in a way that justified the purchase of a 5000lb car. You should have bought a used Camry instead of insisting on your slave labor Tesla. 

Secondly, that is not how lithium batteries work.

Thirdly, you clearly have no idea how many teslas are totalled out instead of repaired because getting parts is impossible unless you're buying second hand parts. If you want a car that has an "autopilot" that drives full speed into parked firetrucks killing dozens of people, go for it. Just be aware of how many slaves had to extract that lithium from a nine in Africa every time you step on the pedal. I'll happily keep driving my slave labor free vehicle.

@Gigachad854Unfortunately I don’t own a Model S and you don’t know me.
The Model S was originally designed around 2008 to be a lot of things but primarily a full sized car that seats six.
I think the Taycan is a great car. But they are very different cars. Taycan seats four and is primarily designed for performance. Still it took Porsche over 10 years to catch up to Tesla.

@markplott4820  Not 100% sure what you're trying to say here, but in 2024 the average debt in America is $104k.

And if you mean 7500 in tax liability. That equates to an agi of $55k, so about $69,000 in earnings. The National average salary in 2024 is $63,000. Just shy of that.

It's not a great investment to be buying a car that cost about what you make in a year though. That said, in 2018 when the Model 3 first became available, I did exactly that and paid it off in two years. So who am I to judge people's choices.

@Battleneter 
…or 20 minutes while I grab a coffee and take a leak….either way. 🤣🤣🤣
60k in 2 years for me with 7 out of state desert trips. I know. You obviously have no clue.

@TERM-n6kYou “lost” that much due to buying at peak prices. I spoke with someone who financed a Honda Pilot for $68k in 2022. A *Honda* Pilot, not an Acura MDX. Needless to say, he is massively underwater on his car loan.

Teslas experienced abnormal depreciation rates due to a double whammy: Tesla repeatedly raised prices throughout 2021 and into 2022, meaning they had to reduce prices throughout 2023. Then, federal tax credit was reintroduced to 3/Y. IMO, Model 3 had no business starting at $47k *before* taxes and fees. The depreciation that we recently saw won’t be seen again for a long time. 

It sucks that cars from various brands experienced massive depreciation over the past 2 years or so, causing many people to be underwater on their loans. But that should teach people not to bite off more than they can chew. At the end of the day, cars are depreciating assets, not investments. Either buy with cash, or put down at least 35% before getting into a loan. A car losing its value ideally should not significantly impact your net worth.
